---
import Layout from "@components/Layout.astro";
import PageHeader from "@components/PageHeader.astro";
import CardAnalysis from "@components/CardAnalysis.astro";
import { Card } from "webcoreui/astro";

---

<Layout
    title="Replenish Sideboard Guide & Analysis - Premodern MTG"
    image="/src/assets/banners/opalescence.jpeg"
    description="The definitive Replenish sideboard guide for Premodern. Card-by-card analysis, tier-ranked tournament staples, and metagame bullets from 180+ post-ban decklists."
>
    <PageHeader
        title="Replenish Sideboard Guide"
        description="A card-by-card look at the Premodern Replenish sideboard: the Tier 1 staples you always want, the metagame bullets worth knowing, and the flexible slots you tune to your local field."
        imageSrc="/src/assets/banners/opalescence.jpeg"
        imageAlt="Opalescence"
        imageCaption="Opalescence by John Avon"
        isCard={true}
    />
    <Card title="Metagame Data Methodology" titleTag="h2">
        <p class="section-intro">
            Every tier ranking and inclusion percentage in this guide comes from
            going through <strong>180 winning Premodern tournament decklists</strong
            > (pulled from MTGDecks). To keep the numbers relevant to how the
            format plays now, we only counted events held <strong
                >after the ban</strong
            > (once <span class="card-hover" role="button" tabindex="0"
                data-card-name="Parallax Tide">Parallax Tide</a
            > left the format). Cutting out the old "Tide-era" lists is what shows
            you what modern lists actually agree on: you need to protect your mana,
            and you need post-board threats you can turn the corner with.
        </p>
    </Card>



    <Card title="Tier 1: Core Tournament Staples" titleTag="h2" id="tier-1">
        <p class="section-intro">
            These are the cards that hold competitive Replenish sideboards
            together. The post-ban data backs it up: they answer problems that
            come up across the whole format, and they belong in just about every
            75.
        </p>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Exalted Angel"
            image="creatures/exalted_angel.jpg"
            badge="2–3 Side · 95.6% Inclusion"
        >
            <p>
                Your go-to post-board threat, and you'll bring it in for almost
                every non-combo matchup. Against red, even flipping it down as a
                morph blocker that eats a burn spell saves you real life. Against
                control, it forces them to have removal they've often trimmed
                after Game 1. Against mana-denial decks (<a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Terravore">Terrageddon</a
                >), getting an early Angel clock going usually talks them out of
                casting <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Armageddon">Armageddon</a
                > at all.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Why the flip matters:</strong> opponents almost always cut
                spot removal for enchantment sweepers and graveyard hate (<a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Aura of Silence">Aura of Silence</a
                >, <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Tormod's Crypt">Tormod's Crypt</a
                >), so an early morph flip runs away with games against UW Control,
                Rock, and Ponza while they sit there holding hate they can't use.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Meddling Mage"
            image="creatures/meddling_mage.webp"
            badge="3–4 Side · 92.2% Inclusion"
        >
            <p>
                One of the best sideboard cards in the post-ban format. Over 92%
                of sideboards run 3 or 4 copies (and another 10% play some
                maindeck), so <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Meddling Mage">Meddling Mage</a
                > is about as close to a consensus card for Games 2 and 3 as it
                gets. It does two jobs at once: it names the one spell that would
                blow you out, and it chips in on a fast clock next to Exalted Angel
                once your opponent has trimmed their removal.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>What to name:</strong> against graveyard hate, name <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Tormod's Crypt">Tormod's Crypt</a
                > or <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Phyrexian Furnace">Phyrexian Furnace</a
                > so your Replenish loop can go off clean. Against control, naming <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Counterspell">Counterspell</a
                > or their enchantment sweeper means the combo resolves when you
                need it to. For the full list of what to name in each matchup, check
                the <a href="/matchups" class="guide-link">Matchup Matrix</a
                >.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Tsabo's Web"
            image="artifacts/tsabos_web.jpg"
            badge="1–3 Side · 77.8% Inclusion"
        >
            <p>
                Some pilots play <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Tsabo's Web">Tsabo's Web</a
                > maindeck as general disruption, but over 77% keep it in the
                sideboard for Games 2 and 3, when the grind really sets in. After
                board, aggressive decks (Goblins, Rock, Ponza) lean even harder on
                mana denial and land taxation to choke your four-mana setup turns
                before you can deal with their graveyard hate. Web shuts that down,
                keeps your mana on schedule, and replaces itself with a card.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Defense Grid"
            image="artifacts/defense_grid.jpg"
            badge="0–3 Side · 52.8% Inclusion"
        >
            <p>
                Your best tool against counterspell decks. Making them pay three
                extra mana to cast anything on your turn shuts off their
                countermagic long enough to land a four-mana combo piece. It also
                plays nicely with cycling <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Decree of Silence">Decree of Silence</a
                > on their end step.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Hydroblast / Blue Elemental Blast"
            image="instants/hydroblast.jpg"
            badge="2–4 Side · 76.1% Inclusion"
        >
            <p>
                The cleanest one-mana answer to fast red starts and red sideboard
                cards. It counters an incoming <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Pyroblast"
                    >Pyroblast / Red Elemental Blast</a
                > aimed at your blue combo pieces, kills a turn-one <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Goblin Lackey">Goblin Lackey</a
                > before it drops a lethal board into play, and answers
                back-breaking enchantment hate like <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Anarchy">Anarchy</a
                >. One mana, always useful.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Seal of Cleansing / Disenchant"
            image="enchantments/seal_of_cleansing.webp"
            badge="1–3 Side · 50.0% Inclusion"
        >
            <p>
                <strong>The Seal/Disenchant Split:</strong> In modern lists,
                <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
                > is the default answer to artifacts and enchantments after board
                (50% of sideboards, 97.8% of maindecks), because it comes back with
                <a role="button" tabindex="0" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Replenish"
                    >Replenish</a
                > and becomes a 2/2 under <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Opalescence">Opalescence</a
                >. The catch is <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Aura of Silence">Aura of Silence</a
                >, which is everywhere now—if Seal is your only answer, that
                two-mana tax lands and sticks. Keeping 1 or 2 <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Disenchant">Disenchant</a
                > (3.9% inclusion) gives you an instant-speed answer that ignores
                Aura's tax entirely.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Abeyance"
            image="instants/abeyance.jpg"
            badge="1–2 Side · 42.2% SB / 72.8% Main"
        >
            <p>
                For the 42.2% of pilots who keep <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Abeyance">Abeyance</a
                > in the sideboard, it does more than just protect the combo in
                Games 2 and 3. When opponents bring in fast graveyard hate after
                Game 1 (<a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Tormod's Crypt">Tormod's Crypt</a
                >, <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Withered Wretch">Withered Wretch</a
                >, or <a role="button" tabindex="0" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Scour"
                    >Scour</a
                >), extra copies let you play a disruption game on their upkeep.
                Cast it on their turn and you lock down their activated hate and
                tie up the mana they'd use to react—enough of a window to land a
                threat like <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Exalted Angel">Exalted Angel</a
                > and take over.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Wrath of God"
            image="sorceries/wrath_of_god.webp"
            badge="20.0% Inclusion"
        >
            <p>
                Your reset button against go-wide tribal decks and fast midrange
                boards. It's climbed to 20% post-ban inclusion for a reason: in
                fields full of Elves, Goblins, or GW aggro, spot removal alone
                isn't enough, and a sweeper buys you the turn or two you need to
                set up the enchantment lock.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>
    </Card>

    <Card
        title="Tier 2: High-Leverage Metagame Bullets"
        titleTag="h2"
        id="tier-2"
    >
        <p class="section-intro">
            Targeted answers for specific decks and the hate they bring in after
            Game 1. <strong>So why aren't these in every list?</strong> Tier 1 cards
            fix problems that show up across a lot of matchups; Tier 2 cards are
            narrow, and you only want them when your local field calls for them (a
            room full of red aggro or mana-denial decks, say). You've only got 15
            slots, and every one you spend on a narrow bullet is one you're not
            spending on your main plan.
        </p>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Anti-Red Defense: Sphere of Law vs. Warmth vs. CoP: Red"
            image="enchantments/sphere_of_law.jpg"
            badge="Theory & Comparison · 20.0% Inclusion"
            collapsible={true}
        >
            <p>
                Which red defense you want comes down to how much mana you can
                spare and what kind of damage you expect to face:
            </p>
            <ul class="analysis-list">
                <li>
                    <strong>Sphere of Law (Recommended):</strong> The best four-mana
                    option against both Goblins and Sligh, and the most-played of the
                    three at 20% post-ban. Shaving two damage off <em>every</em>
                    source builds a wall you don't have to keep paying for. The
                    downside: red decks will bring in <a
                        role="button" tabindex="0"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Anarchy">Anarchy</a
                    > against it, so have countermagic ready.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Warmth:</strong> Great against spell-heavy Sligh, where
                    two life per red spell just erases their burn. Against Goblins
                    it falls apart—their creatures deal combat damage faster than the
                    lifegain can keep up, so it does very little.
                </li>
                <li class="no-margin-bottom">
                    <strong>Circle of Protection: Red:</strong> Protects you against
                    anything, but it's too <em>mana hungry.</em> We're a proactive
                    combo-control deck, and our mana is already stretched thin
                    cycling <a
                        role="button" tabindex="0"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Attunement">Attunement</a
                    > and running <a
                        role="button" tabindex="0"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Frantic Search">Frantic Search</a
                    >—we can't sit back untapped like a draw-go deck. Paying to
                    prevent damage every turn just gets in the way of setting up the
                    combo, so CoP is the weakest of the three here.
                </li>
            </ul>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Chill"
            image="enchantments/chill.jpg"
            badge="2–3 Side · 10.0% Inclusion"
            collapsible={true}
        >
            <p>
                <a role="button" tabindex="0" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Chill">Chill</span> is
                fantastic against classic Sligh and Burn—doubling the cost of their
                red spells basically stops the burn dead—but it has a real problem
                against Goblins.
            </p>
            <p>
                Goblins often just drops a turn-one <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Goblin Lackey">Goblin Lackey</a
                > before you can even cast Chill. Once Lackey connects, they're
                putting creatures straight onto the battlefield without casting
                anything, and your two-mana tax does nothing. It's an enchantment,
                so discarded copies come back with <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a
                >, but don't lean on it as your only Goblins plan—back it up with
                one-mana removal.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Orim's Chant"
            image="instants/orims_chant.webp"
            badge="1–2 Side · 13.3% Inclusion"
            collapsible={true}
        >
            <p>
                Back in the Parallax Tide era, <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Orim's Chant">Orim's Chant</a
                > was a top protection card. When you were racing to resolve a
                turn-three or turn-four Tide and blow up five of their lands in a
                5-for-1, spending one mana to clear the way—even down a card—was
                well worth it against blue decks.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Why Abeyance took over post-ban:</strong> once Parallax Tide
                was banned, that changed. Modern Replenish isn't racing a turn-four
                deadline against blue decks that put you on no clock, so saving one
                mana doesn't matter much anymore. And <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Abeyance">Abeyance</a
                > just does more: it replaces itself with a card, it can shut off an
                activated ability (freezing an <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Tormod's Crypt">Tormod's Crypt</a
                > or <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Withered Wretch">Withered Wretch</a
                > right before you combo), and it buys great tempo in the mirror and
                against Enchantress. These days Chant is only worth it when you
                specifically need the cheapest possible upkeep delay against fast
                combo.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Annul"
            image="instants/annul.jpg"
            badge="1–2 Side · Instant Answer"
            collapsible={true}
        >
            <p>
                A flexible one-mana counter that deals with problem permanents on
                the stack—stopping a taxing effect before it locks up your mana, or
                answering something when waiting to crack an on-board <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
                > would be too slow.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>What it hits in Premodern:</strong> keeping Annul in your 15
                gives you clean 1-for-1s against a long list of format staples: <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Gloom">Gloom</a
                >, <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Sphere of Resistance"
                    >Sphere of Resistance</a
                >, opposing <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
                >, <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Aura of Silence">Aura of Silence</a
                >, <a role="button" tabindex="0" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Parallax Wave"
                    >Parallax Wave</a
                > (crucial in mirror battles), <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Standstill">Standstill</a
                >, <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Decree of Silence">Decree of Silence</a
                >, <a role="button" tabindex="0" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Aluren"
                    >Aluren</a
                >, <a role="button" tabindex="0" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Dream Halls"
                    >Dream Halls</a
                >, <a role="button" tabindex="0" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Mox Diamond"
                    >Mox Diamond</a
                >, <a role="button" tabindex="0" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Cursed Totem"
                    >Cursed Totem</a
                >, <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Phyrexian Furnace">Phyrexian Furnace</a
                >, <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Tormod's Crypt">Tormod's Crypt</a
                >, <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Sterling Grove">Sterling Grove</a
                >, <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Zombie Infestation">Zombie Infestation</a
                >, <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Phyrexian Devourer">Phyrexian Devourer</a
                >, <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Altar of Dementia">Altar of Dementia</a
                >, and <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Survival of the Fittest"
                    >Survival of the Fittest</a
                >.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Alter Reality"
            image="instants/alter_reality.jpg"
            badge="0–1 Side · Regional Tech"
            collapsible={true}
        >
            <p>
                Narrow regional tech aimed squarely at <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Gloom">Gloom</a
                > out of black disruption decks. Change the word "White" to "Black"
                on their resolved Gloom and the tax now falls on their own spells
                instead of yours. It has Flashback, so it still works even if it
                gets discarded, and you can tutor for it with <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Intuition">Intuition</a
                >. You can also use it to strip a creature's protection from white,
                or to change the color on your own <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Circle of Protection: Red">Circle of Protection: Red</a
                > if you're running it.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Tier 3: Niche & Modular Technology" titleTag="h2" id="tier-3">
        <p class="section-intro">
            Specialized tools and niche slots that show up in under 10% of post-ban
            lists. <strong>Why so little play?</strong> Most of these are either
            bullets for rare matchups, or old pre-ban ideas that flexible 4-ofs like
            Meddling Mage and Exalted Angel have simply outclassed. They can still be
            right—but only if you have a clear read on your local field.
        </p>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Tormod's Crypt"
            image="artifacts/tormods_crypt.jpg"
            badge="0–3 Side · Surgical Tech"
            collapsible={true}
        >
            <p>
                The zero-mana standard for graveyard hate. Big against fast
                Reanimator and the Replenish mirror. In the mirror, it's all about
                timing: leave your Crypt on the table and only sacrifice it in
                response to their <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a
                > resolving or an <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Attunement">Attunement</a
                > activation, so you wipe their setup and keep your own graveyard
                intact. It also comes in against Terrageddon now and then to shrink
                their <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Terravore">Terravore</a
                >, though that's usually not worth a slot.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Nevinyrral's Disk"
            image="artifacts/nevinyrrals_disk.jpg"
            badge="0–2 Side · Universal Reset"
            collapsible={true}
        >
            <p>
                The best catch-all reset against permanent-based locks, wide
                midrange boards, and resolved anti-combo artifacts. In long grindy
                games (The Rock, UW Control), popping an active Disk wipes their
                creatures and hate pieces and drops your own enchantments into the
                graveyard at the same time—setting up a follow-up <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a
                > onto an empty board.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>The catch:</strong> Disk enters tapped, so it needs a full
                turn before you can use it. That's a window for them to kill it at
                instant speed, and against a deck with artifact hate you can end up
                paying four mana for nothing and falling way behind.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Light of Day"
            image="enchantments/light_of_day.jpg"
            badge="1–2 Side · Silver Bullet"
            collapsible={true}
        >
            <p>
                A near-total lock against black creature decks. Against Suicide
                Black, Psychatog, or aggressive Rock builds, a resolved <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Light of Day">Light of Day</a
                > means their black creatures can't attack or block, full stop. And
                since it's a four-mana enchantment, it comes back with <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a
                > if they strip it with early discard.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Spiritual Focus"
            image="enchantments/spiritual_focus.jpg"
            badge="0–1 Side · Anti-Discard Tech"
            collapsible={true}
        >
            <p>
                A defensive enchantment that punishes black discard decks by
                drawing you two cards every time they make you pitch a card with <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Gerrard's Verdict">Gerrard's Verdict</a
                > or <a role="button" tabindex="0" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Duress"
                    >Duress</a
                >. Some longtime pilots like it for out-grinding attrition decks,
                but it's slow and passive, and it tends to be too little too late
                against fast, tempo-driven starts.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Null Rod"
            image="artifacts/null_rod.jpg"
            badge="0–1 Side · Anti-Artifact"
            collapsible={true}
        >
            <p>
                A clean anti-artifact lock that shuts off enemy mana rocks and,
                more importantly, activated graveyard-hate artifacts like <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Tormod's Crypt">Tormod's Crypt</a
                >, <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Phyrexian Furnace">Phyrexian Furnace</a
                >, and <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Nevinyrral's Disk">Nevinyrral's Disk</a
                >, and it shuts off the <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Phyrexian Devourer">Phyrexian Devourer</a
                > combo entirely.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Powder Keg"
            image="artifacts/powder_keg.jpg"
            badge="0–1 Side · Flexible Sweeper"
            collapsible={true}
        >
            <p>
                A flexible artifact sweeper that clears out low-curve creature
                swarms, Moxen, and token boards—and it does it without asking for
                double-colored mana.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Sacred Ground"
            image="enchantments/sacred_ground.jpg"
            badge="0–1 Side · Anti-LD Fortress"
            collapsible={true}
        >
            <p>
                Your best anti-land-destruction card against Ponza and Terrageddon,
                turning their
                <a role="button" tabindex="0" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Wasteland"
                    >Wastelands</a
                > and <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Stone Rain">Stone Rains</a
                > into blanks—and since it's an enchantment, it fits right into your
                recursion loops.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Aura of Silence"
            image="enchantments/aura_of_silence.jpg"
            badge="0–1 Side · Asymmetric Tax"
            collapsible={true}
        >
            <p>
                A one-sided tax and a recurring answer to artifacts and
                enchantments that really hurts the mirror and Sol-land artifact
                decks. It can sacrifice itself for removal and then come back with <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a
                >, so it's both disruption and a repeatable answer. It's at its best
                in the mirror, against Enchantress, and against Stiflenought. The
                three-mana cost makes it a touch slower than <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
                >—which is why it sees less play overall—but plenty of pilots run
                1–2 in the board for those specific matchups.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Library of Leng"
            image="artifacts/library_of_leng.jpg"
            badge="0–1 Side · Hand Sculpting"
            collapsible={true}
        >
            <p>
                Protects a hand you've worked to set up against targeted discard,
                since you can send discarded cards to the top of your library
                instead of the graveyard when it helps—and it lets you ignore the
                seven-card hand limit during big draw turns.
            </p>
            <p>
                On top of being a hard answer to discard, it has nice synergy with <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Attunement">Attunement</a
                >: you can stack the cards you discard right on top of your library
                for the next few turns. It's an interesting option that's worth more
                testing.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Mana Short"
            image="instants/mana_short.jpg"
            badge="0–1 Side · Tap-Down Bullet"
            collapsible={true}
        >
            <p>
                A brutal end-step tap-down against counterspell decks: strip all
                their blue mana on their turn, then untap and combo off on your main
                phase with no countermagic left to stop you.
            </p>
            <p>
                It's strong, but it's slower and costs more mana than <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Abeyance">Abeyance</a
                >, which is why Abeyance is the go-to protection spell in most modern
                lists.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Arcane Laboratory"
            image="enchantments/arcane_laboratory.jpg"
            badge="0–1 Side · Anti-Combo"
            collapsible={true}
        >
            <p>
                A hard stop against decks that chain spells together—storm and the
                like—since it limits everyone to one spell per turn, and it comes
                back with Replenish if they blow it up. A neat silver bullet that's
                especially good against Stiflenought and storm decks.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>
    </Card>

